      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "In childhood obesity research, the appearance of height loss, or \"shrinkage,\" indicates measurement error. It is unclear whether a common response--excluding \"shrinkers\" from analysis--reduces bias."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Using data from the National Longitudinal Study of Adolescent Health, we sampled 816 female adolescents (≥17 years) who had attained adult height by 1996 and for whom adult height was consistently measured in 2001 and 2008 (\"gold-standard\" height). We estimated adolescent obesity prevalence and the association of maternal education with adolescent obesity under 3 conditions: excluding shrinkers (for whom gold-standard height was less than recorded height in 1996), retaining shrinkers, and retaining shrinkers but substituting their gold-standard height."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "When we estimated obesity prevalence, excluding shrinkers decreased precision without improving validity. When we regressed obesity on maternal education, excluding shrinkers produced less valid and less precise estimates."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In some circumstances, ignoring shrinkage is a better strategy than excluding shrinkers."
        }
      ]},
